      "text": "To determine the total surface area of the right pyramid, we need to calculate the area of the base and the four triangular faces.\n\nFirst, let's find the area of the square base:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{base}} = \\text{side length}^2 = 10^2 = 100 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of one of the triangular faces. The slant height of the pyramid can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem. The height of the pyramid is 12 cm, and the distance from the center of the base to the midpoint of one of the base sides is half the side length of the base, which is:\n\\[\n\\text{distance from center to midpoint} = \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\, \\text{cm}\n\\]\n\nThe slant height forms a right triangle with the height of the pyramid and the distance from the center of the base to the midpoint of a side. The slant height \\( l \\) can be found using:\n\\[\nl = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\, \\text{cm}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can calculate the area of one triangular face:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{triangle}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{slant height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 13 = 65 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n\\]\n\nSince there are four triangular faces, the total area of the triangular faces is:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{triangles}} = 4 \\times 65 = 260 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n\\]\n\nFinally, the total surface area of the pyramid is the sum of the area of the base and the areas of the four triangular faces:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{total}} = A_{\\text{base}} + A_{\\text{triangles}} = 100 + 260 = 360 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n\\]\n\nThus, the total surface area of the pyramid is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{360}\n\\]",
