      "text": "To find the total surface area of the right pyramid, we need to calculate the area of each face and the area of the base. We have sidelong rectangular faces, a square face and its 4 triangular faces.\n\nBase:\nThe area of a square is side length squared. Therefore, the area of the square face is $10^2= 100$ square centimeters.\n\nRectangular Faces:\nThe area of each rectangular face is the product of the base length and the height. Since the base has a length of 10 cm and the height is 12 cm, each rectangular face has an area of $12\\cdot10= 120$ square centimeters.\n\nTotal Rectangular Face Area:\nThe total area of all 4 face rectangles is $4\\cdot120= 480$ square centimeters.\n\nSquare Face Area:\nTo find the area of the square face, we need to find the length of one of its sides. The length of the midsection of a diagonal of a square can be found by dividing its side 10 cm by square root of 2. Therefore, the length of a side of the square pyramid's base is $\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{2}} = 5\\sqrt{2}$ cm. Multiplying this length by itself, we find the area of the square face is $(5\\sqrt{2})^2 = 50$ square centimeters.\n\nTotal Surface Area:\nFinally, to find the total surface area of the pyramid, we add up all the areas of the faces. This means adding the areas of all the rectangles and the square area, giving us the total surface area of the pyramid to be $\\boxed{580}$ square centimeters.",
